# Vietnamese bound-phrase emphasis

Vietnamese compound words are often two syllables (`im lặng`, `hình ảnh`). They are listed in `VIETNAMESE_BOUND_PHRASES` in `features/kinetic-text/lib/text-language/vietnamese-phrases.ts`.

**Never emphasize only part of a bound phrase.** If scoring picks one syllable, call `expandEmphasisToBoundPhrases` so every syllable in that phrase is emphasized together.

When adding new two-syllable Vietnamese words, add both accented and unaccented forms to `VIETNAMESE_BOUND_PHRASES`.

Bad: only `lặng` glows in `… im lặng.`

Good: both `im` and `lặng` glow together with the **same** emphasis variant (halo, sweep, etc.) — use `getBoundPhraseEmphasisSeed` / `getBoundPhraseStartIndex`, not per-syllable variant keys.

## Compound-word integrity

Vietnamese compound words are space-separated syllable pairs (`học sinh` = student, `bệnh viện` = hospital) and most are not in `VIETNAMESE_BOUND_PHRASES`, so scoring can still select one syllable and split the compound.

**Vietnamese compound words must never be partially emphasized.** The `repairSplitCompoundEmphasis` guard (in `features/kinetic-text/lib/text-language/vietnamese-phrases.ts`) ensures that if one syllable of a likely compound word is emphasized, adjacent syllables are included. **Any new emphasis selection path must call this guard for Vietnamese text** (`isLikelyVietnameseText`), after `expandEmphasisToBoundPhrases`.

Guard mechanics: a pair is "likely compound" when both syllables are content syllables (at least 2 letters, outside the `VIETNAMESE_STOP_WORDS` set) and no sentence/clause punctuation separates them. Each originally selected index adds at most one syllable left and one right (pairs only, never chains); the returned indexes are deduplicated and ascending.

Bad: only `sinh` glows in `… là học sinh giỏi.`

Good: `học`, `sinh` (and, when adjacent, `giỏi`) glow together.

## Upstream data annotations

Deck-sourced vocabulary words may carry `emphasis: string[]` — crawler-produced Vietnamese emphasis phrases (the crawler's internal `emphasisVi` converted to the deck field `emphasis`). Both emphasis APIs accept an optional `dataEmphasis?: string[]` argument: `getEmphasizedWordIndexes` (`features/post-player/lib/feed-emphasis.ts`) and `getPreviewEmphasizedWordIndexes` (`features/kinetic-text/components/preview-emphasis.ts`).

**When at least one annotation occurs in the current text, exactly those matches win over poetic-phrase and score-based selection.** Match annotations with `getDataEmphasisWordIndexes` (`features/kinetic-text/lib/text-language/data-emphasis.ts`): consecutive whole-token runs, NFC-normalized, case-insensitive, punctuation stripped at token boundaries, **diacritics preserved** — `những` must never match `nhưng`, and `nền` must never match `nên`. Never reuse the accent-stripped `normalizeVietnameseToken` for data emphasis matching. Phrases containing punctuation-only tokens are skipped.

Data matches still pass through `expandEmphasisToBoundPhrases` and `repairSplitCompoundEmphasis` (Vietnamese text only), so a partial-syllable annotation like `sinh` heals into `học sinh`.

When no annotation matches the current text, fall back to the existing scoring path unchanged. Legacy decks without `emphasis` and non-vocabulary social posts never pass `dataEmphasis`, so their behavior is identical.

Vocabulary cards render several stage texts per word: pass the word's annotations to every stage (`VocabularyStage` → `WordSequenceText` `dataEmphasis`) and let exact matching decide — never force-highlight an annotation that does not occur in the current stage text.

Bad: annotation `tĩnh` glows alone in `… sự bình tĩnh …` while scoring picks another word, or `những` glows in a sentence containing `nhưng`.

Good: annotations `bình tĩnh` glow as a run; the letters-count stage (which contains neither phrase) keeps its current scoring emphasis.

Emphasis must not create oversized spaces. Phrase containers retain intrinsic width and use a single `0.24em` gap, including inside shared frames. Never center a full-width highlighted phrase beside other words in a non-wrapping line. Look up emphasis with each word's original page index, not its segment-local position.
